    <h2 class="article-h3">Marks that last a lifetime</h2>
    <blockquote class="article-quote">"Things that we do without lust of result are the purest actions we shall ever take." — attributed to Alan Moore</blockquote>

    <div class="article-body">
      <p>Pornography can affect children in several ways, including:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><b>Confusion about sexuality:</b> pornography presents a distorted and unrealistic view of sex, which can lead children to a confused and mistaken understanding of sexuality.</li>
        <li><b>Pressure to conform:</b> children may feel pressure to conform to sexual behaviours shown in pornography, even when they are inappropriate or harmful.</li>
        <li><b>Higher risk of harmful sexual behaviour:</b> exposure can lead children to try sexual behaviours early, including taking part in activities without consent.</li>
        <li><b>Higher risk of sexual victimisation:</b> children who watch pornography are at greater risk of sexual abuse, as they may become targets of predators who use such content as a form of "grooming".</li>
        <li><b>Anxiety, depression and self-image problems:</b> it can make children feel inadequate about their looks and abilities, leading to low self-esteem.</li>
        <li><b>Wrong lessons about intimacy and relationships:</b> pornography teaches that sex is only physical and empty of emotional connection, distorting children's view of affection and healthy relationships.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>It is important to remember that pornography is no healthy substitute for proper sex education and open, honest communication between parents and children. Parents should be aware that their children may be exposed to this content, especially in the digital age, and should take steps to teach them about sexuality and guide them to browse safely online.</p>
    </div>

